    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Combined flexion and twisting under fatigue is a common trigger of back pain; keep the reps controlled and stop the set when form slips (clinical opinion).",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Neck pain: cross the arms on the chest and turn the chest.",
        "Smaller twist and range."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Crunch.",
        "Arms reaching across to the outside of the knee (no hands behind the head)."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slow 3 s lowering with a 2 s hold.",
        "Bicycle crunch.",
        "Feet off the floor (knees at 90/90)."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "obliques_r": "External oblique on the side of the turning shoulder and internal oblique on the other side rotate the curling trunk: external oblique rose to about 36% MVC in a cross (twisting) curl-up versus 29% in a straight curl-up (Konrad 2001); internal oblique and transversus were more active turning one way and external oblique the other (Crommert 2021).",
      "obliques_l": "External oblique on the side of the turning shoulder and internal oblique on the other side rotate the curling trunk: external oblique rose to about 36% MVC in a cross (twisting) curl-up versus 29% in a straight curl-up (Konrad 2001); internal oblique and transversus were more active turning one way and external oblique the other (Crommert 2021).",
      "core": "Rectus abdominis still curls the trunk: about 56% MVC in the cross curl-up (Konrad 2001), and it did not change with the twist direction (Crommert 2021).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Stay quiet and only steady the bent legs (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Stay quiet and only steady the bent legs (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
